{"finish_reason":"stop","kind":"response","reasoning":"complete. Summarizing concisely: changed items, data format, validation, backward compatibility, tests performed. Note the scope: updating existing fields is not supported — follow-up.\n","seq":857,"t":104819,"text":"\n\nDone. Two new fields, still one file, stdlib only:\n\n- `todo.py add <text> [--priority high|normal|low] [--due YYYY-MM-DD]` — priority defaults to `normal`; `--due` is optional and omitted from the record when not given\n- `todo.py list` — now prints `priority:` always and `due:` when present, e.g. `2. file report  priority: high  due: 2025-02-01`\n\nDetails worth knowing:\n\n- Switched from manual `sys.argv` parsing to `argparse` (still stdlib) — it gives `--help` for free and handles quoting/multi-word text via `nargs=\"+\"`.\n- `parse_due` in `todo.py` validates the date with `datetime.strptime(value, \"%Y-%m-%d\")`, so `2025-13-99` is rejected with a clear message.\n- `--priority` uses `choices`, so `urgent` fails with the valid list.\n- Old entries without the fields are tolerated: `list_todos` reads priority via `t.get(\"priority\", \"normal\")`, so no migration needed.\n\nTested: default add, high+due add, low add, invalid date (exit 2), invalid priority (exit 2), and a legacy entry missing both fields (shows `normal`, no due). I reset `todos.json` to `[]` afterward.\n\nOut of scope here, flagging as a follow-up: there's no way to change priority/due on an existing task yet — if you want that, an `update <id>` command is the natural next step.","timings":{"cache_n":7168,"draft_n":847,"draft_n_accepted":279,"predicted_ms":4330.0,"predicted_n":400,"prompt_ms":150.0,"prompt_n":121},"to_request":733}
