{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Adding a `done <id>` command: mark the task as complete (set `done: true`). And `list` sorts by priority (high first), then by due date.\n\nDecisions:\n- `done <id>`: find task by id, set `\"done\": true`. Error if id doesn't exist or isn't an integer. What about marking something already done? Idempotent — maybe say \"already done\" or just mark it. Let's keep it simple: mark it, print confirmation. Should I handle non-integer id → error message.\n- Data: add `\"done\": true` only when marked (don't clutter every record with `done: false`). Use `t.get(\"done\", False)` when reading.\n- list: sort by priority high > normal > low, then by due date. What about tasks without a due date? Convention: sort by due date, but where do undated ones go? Reasonable: no due date goes last within the same priority (or first?). Hmm. \"Sort by priority (high first), then by due date\" — undated tasks: let's put them after dated ones within the same priority (missing = last). Or ascending with missing at the end. I'll do this: within the same priority, sort by due date ascending, undated last.\n- Also: should completed tasks be sorted together with the others, or should completed ones be shown last? Not specified. Keep it simple: sort only by priority then due date as requested. Display a marker on completed tasks, e.g. `[x]` vs `[ ]`. The list should show completion status — otherwise done is meaningless. Show a `done` marker: e.g. `1. buy milk  priority: normal  [done]` or use the checkbox `x`/` `. Display like this:\n  `1. buy milk  priority: normal  due: 2025-02-01  [x]`\n  Hmm, or prefix with `[x] 1. ...`. I'll add `  done` at the end… Let's use a checkbox at the end: `[x]` if done, `[ ]` otherwise. Clear and easy to parse.\n\nSorting priority: rank map {\"high\": 0, \"normal\": 1, \"low\": 2}.\nDue date: string comparison of YYYY-MM-DD works lexicographically = chronological order. Undated: use a sort key that puts them last: key = (priority_rank, has_due ? 0 : 1, due or \"\"). Actually more simply: key = (rank, due or \"9999-99-99\")? This makes undated last. But is that the desired order? \"Then by due date\" — undated has no due date, so last within the same priority feels right. I'll note this choice.\n\nWait — should completed tasks be excluded from list? No — list all, show the marker.\n\ndone command: id must be an int. argparse type=int, but negative values or zero → not found → error \"no task with id N\".\n\nLet me write the code.\n\n```python\ndef mark_done(task_id):\n    todos = load_todos()\n    for t in todos:\n        if t.get(\"id\") == task_id:\n            if t.get(\"done\"):\n                print(f\"task {task_id} is already done\")\n                return\n            t[\"done\"] = True\n            save_todos(todos)\n            print(f\"done: {t['text']}\")\n            return\n    sys.exit(f\"error: no task with id {task_id}\")\n```\n\nSorting list:\n\n```python\nPRIORITY_RANK = {\"high\": 0, \"normal\": 1, \"low\": 2}\n\ndef list_todos():\n    todos = load_todos()\n    if not todos:\n        print(\"no tasks\")\n        return\n    ordered = sorted(\n        todos,\n        key=lambda t: (\n            PRIORITY_RANK.get(t.get(\"priority\", \"normal\"), 1),\n            t.get(\"due\") or \"9999-99-99\",\n        ),\n    )\n    for t in ordered:\n        mark = \"[x]\" if t.get(\"done\") else \"[ ]\"\n        line = f\"{mark} {t['id']}. {t['text']}  priority: {t.get('priority', 'normal')}\"\n        if \"due\" in t:\n            line += f\"  due: {t['due']}\"\n        print(line)\n```\n\nAdd to argparse:\n\n```python\np_done = sub.add_parser(\"done\", help=\"mark a task done\")\np_done.add_argument(\"id\", type=int)\n```\n\nAnd main:\n```python\nif args.cmd == \"add\":\n    ...\nelif args.cmd == \"done\":\n    mark_done(args.id)\nelse:\n    list_todos()\n```\n\nTests:\n- Add a few tasks with different priorities/dates, verify ordering.\n- done on existing id, done on nonexistent id, done on non-integer (argparse error), double done.\n- Undated ones come last within the same priority.\n\nAlright, let's implement.\n","seq":1309,"t":151755,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":7680,"draft_n":2457,"draft_n_accepted":907,"predicted_ms":12710.0,"predicted_n":1258,"prompt_ms":90.0,"prompt_n":51},"to_request":976}
