{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Current state:\n- Priorities: high/normal only. `low` has been removed, and migration happens inside `load_todos()` (already implemented, and the file is rewritten via `save_todos`).\n- What's missing: the `update <id> --priority/--due` subcommand (mentioned twice to the user). Also, according to the follow-up, `undo`/unmark-done is not there either.\n\nThe user's question: \"What priorities did we settle on, and what's still missing from the CLI? Add whatever is missing that we discussed, consistent with those decisions.\"\n\nSo:\n1. Answer: high/normal, low was removed.\n2. What's missing: `update <id> --priority/--due` (discussed twice). `undo`/unmark-done was also mentioned. interview-t4-1 says \"the `update <id>` (and unmark-done) will be implemented only if explicitly requested.\" The user is now explicitly requesting: \"Add whatever is missing that we discussed.\" This is an explicit request.\n\nShould I add both `update` and `undo`? What \"we discussed\" refers to is:\n- The follow-up: \"The `update <id>` command to change priority/due on existing tasks is still unimplemented (mentioned twice to the user). No `undo`/unmark-done either.\"\n- interview-t4-1: \"The `update <id>` (and unmark-done) will be implemented only if explicitly requested.\"\n\n\"Add whatever is missing that we discussed\" — the discussion covered both `update` and unmark-done. The safest interpretation consistent with the decision: implement `update <id> --priority/--due` reusing `parse_due` and `PRIORITIES`. As for `undo`/unmark-done — it was mentioned (\"No `undo`/unmark-done either\"), and interview-t4-1 lumps it together with update as \"only if explicitly requested.\" The user says \"add whatever is missing that we discussed\" — unmark-done was discussed (mentioned to the user twice as part of the same follow-up). Hmm, but the core discussion (interview-t2-1) was specifically about `update <id> --priority/--due`.\n\nLet me think carefully. The follow-up note says: \"The `update <id>` command to change priority/due on existing tasks is still unimplemented (mentioned twice to the user). No `undo`/unmark-done either.\" So both were mentioned to the user. The user is now saying \"add whatever is missing that we discussed\" — that's explicit permission for both.\n\nBut the smallest change that solves the problem is… the user explicitly asked to add \"whatever is missing that we discussed.\" The things discussed are `update` and unmark-done. I'll implement both: `update <id> --priority/--due` and `undo <id>` (unmark done). No wait — let me re-read: \"Add whatever is missing that we discussed, consistent with those decisions.\" \"Those decisions\" = the high/normal decision. So `update` must only accept high/normal (reuse PRIORITIES and parse_due).\n\nAs for unmark-done, it's a separate feature that was mentioned. Adding it is consistent. I think adding both is right, but let me consider: is there a risk of overreach? The user's phrase \"whatever is missing that we discussed\" strongly suggests they want all discussed missing pieces added. Both `update` and `undo` were discussed (the follow-up explicitly lists both, and interview-t4-1 explicitly names both as conditional on being requested). So I'll add both.\n\nDesign:\n\n```python\ndef update(task_id, priority, due):\n    todos = load_todos()\n    for t in todos:\n        if t.get(\"id\") == task_id:\n            if priority is not None:\n                t[\"priority\"] = priority\n            if due is not None:\n                t[\"due\"] = due\n            save_todos(todos)\n            print(f\"updated: {t['text']}\")\n            return\n    sys.exit(f\"error: no task with id {task_id}\")\n```\n\nWait — what if neither --priority nor --due is given? In that case argparse should error: \"error: provide --priority and/or --due\". Or should it just print \"nothing to update\"? Better to require at least one: use a check inside main or the parser. I'll do:\n\n```python\np_update = sub.add_parser(\"update\", help=\"change a task's priority and/or due date\")\np_update.add_argument(\"id\", type=int, help=\"task id\")\np_update.add_argument(\"--priority\", choices=PRIORITIES)\np_update.add_argument(\"--due\", type=parse_due, default=None, help=\"due date YYYY-MM-DD\")\n```\n\nAnd in main:\n```python\nelif args.cmd == \"update\":\n    if args.priority is None and args.due is None:\n        parser.error(\"update requires --priority and/or --due\")\n    update(args.id, args.priority, args.due)\n```\n\nShould `update --due` allow clearing the due date? Not discussed; keep it minimal. What if due is given? Set it. Could also allow `--due \"\"`… no, keep it minimal and consistent with the decision.\n\nAnd `undo`:\n\n```python\ndef undo(task_id):\n    todos = load_todos()\n    for t in todos:\n        if t.get(\"id\") == task_id:\n            if not t.get(\"done\"):\n                print(f\"task {task_id} is not done\")\n                return\n            del t[\"done\"]\n            save_todos(todos)\n            print(f\"undone: {t['text']}\")\n            return\n    sys.exit(f\"error: no task with id {task_id}\")\n```\n\nHmm — should I add `undo`? Let me reconsider. The working set has:\n\n- interview-t2-1 (plan): \"Implement `update <id> --priority/--due` … only if you ask for it\"\n- interview-t4-1 (plan): \"Nothing pending — the scope change is implemented and tested; I would implement `update <id>` (and unmark-done) only if explicitly requested.\"\n- Follow-up: \"The `update <id>` command to change priority/due on existing tasks is still unimplemented (mentioned twice to the user). No `undo`/unmark-done either.\"\n\nThe user's question: \"What's still missing from the CLI? Add whatever is missing that we discussed.\" The discussion explicitly covers both `update` and unmark-done (interview-t4-1 explicitly pairs them). I'll add both. That's the honest reading of \"whatever is missing that we discuss